NUMPY TOPIC 3 — VECTORIZATION
Revision Report
=============================================================

CORE PURPOSE
------------
Vectorization means expressing operations on entire arrays instead
of looping over elements. NumPy operations drop into compiled C/Fortran
and process arrays in one call — typically 50–200× faster than Python
loops. In ML this covers normalization, activation functions, loss
computation, outlier handling, and label encoding — all without a
single loop.

KEY SYNTAX
----------
# Element-wise arithmetic
a + b, a * b, a ** 2, a / scalar

# Universal functions (ufuncs) — elementwise, compiled
np.sqrt(a)
np.log(a)
np.exp(a)
np.abs(a)
np.sign(a)
np.clip(a, low, high)       # both bounds required (use None for open)
np.clip(a, -2, None)        # lower clip only
np.clip(a, None, 10)        # upper clip only

# Conditional replacement
np.where(condition, value_if_true, value_if_false)  # all 3 args required

# Multi-condition branching
np.select(conditions, choices, default=...)         # default always explicit

# Counting elements meeting a condition
np.sum(a < 0)               # counts True values in boolean array

# NOT true vectorization
np.vectorize(fn)            # convenience wrapper — same speed as a loop

PATTERNS
--------
# Z-score normalization — global
X_norm = (X - X.mean()) / X.std()

# Z-score normalization — per column
X_norm = (X - X.mean(axis=0)) / X.std(axis=0)

# Min-max scaling
X_scaled = (X - X.min()) / (X.max() - X.min())

# ReLU activation
relu = np.where(X < 0, 0, X)

# Outlier replacement with clean-values-only statistic
col = X[:, i]
clean_mean = col[col <= threshold].mean()    # exclude outliers first
X[:, i] = np.where(col > threshold, clean_mean, col)

# Missing value imputation
col = X[:, i]
valid_median = np.median(col[col != sentinel])  # exclude sentinels first
X[:, i] = np.where(col == sentinel, valid_median, col)

# Label encoding without loops
conditions = [labels == 'cat', labels == 'dog', labels == 'bird']
encoded = np.select(conditions, [0, 1, 2], default=-1)

# Replace np.vectorize with ufuncs
np.log(np.abs(X) + 1) * np.sign(X)    # fast
np.vectorize(fn)(X)                    # same speed as a loop — avoid

GOTCHAS
-------
1. np.where requires all three arguments
   np.where(cond, a)          → behaves like np.argwhere, returns indices
   np.where(cond, a, b)       → correct elementwise conditional

2. np.clip requires both bounds (use None for open-ended)
   np.clip(a, -2)             → missing upper bound, error
   np.clip(a, -2, None)       → correct lower-only clip

3. np.select default should always be explicit
   np.select(conds, choices)              → implicit default=0, fragile
   np.select(conds, choices, default=-1) → explicit, safe

4. Statistic must be computed on clean values only
   col.mean()                        → contaminated by outliers/sentinels
   col[col <= threshold].mean()      → clean values only, correct

5. np.vectorize is not vectorization
   It is a Python loop with a nicer interface — no speed gain
   Always replace with ufuncs, np.where, or np.select

6. Global vs per-column normalization
   X.mean()          → scalar, global mean across entire matrix
   X.mean(axis=0)    → shape (n_cols,), one mean per column
   Per-column is almost always what you want in ML preprocessing

7. Method calls need parentheses
   a.min    → method reference, prints object descriptor
   a.min()  → actual call, returns value

WHEN TO USE WHAT
----------------
np.where      → single condition, two outcomes
np.select     → multiple conditions, multiple outcomes
np.clip       → clamping to a numeric range
ufuncs        → math transforms (log, exp, sqrt, abs, sign)
np.sum(mask)  → counting elements that meet a condition
np.vectorize  → never for performance; only for quick prototyping
               on small arrays where correctness matters more than speed

